u})$ has a finite number of isomorphism classes, then $\gamma$ is constant.  egin{proof} If by contradiction $\gamma$ is not constant, i.e. the associated homomorphism $\hz	o\pi_{1}(X_{ ar{k}})$ is not trivial, up to replacing $X$ with a finite étale cover we may assume that the composition $\cB_{k}\hz(1)	o\Pi_{X/k}	o\Pi_{X/k}^{m ab}$ is not constant, where $\Pi_{X/k}^{m ab}$ is the abelianized fundamental gerbe, equivalently we may assume that the induced homomorphism $\hz	o\pi_{1}(X_{ ar{k}})^{m ab}$ is not trivial. We may do so because the map of Galois sections associated with a finite étale cover has finite fibers, hence the hypothesis remains true after passing to the covering. Let $J$ be the semi-abelian Jacobian of $X$, it is an extension of an abelian variety $A$ by a torus $T$ and we have a canonical identification $\Pi_{X/k}^{m ab}=\Pi_{J/k}$. We may use the images of preferred section of $\cB_{k}\hz(1)$ to give identifications $\Pi_{J/k}=\cB_{k}\on{T}J$, $\Pi_{A/k}=\cB_{k}\on{T}A$ where $\on{T}J=\projlim_{n}J[n],\on{T}A=\projlim_{n}A[n]$ are the Tate modules; the morphisms of gerbes then correspond to homomorphisms $\hz(1)	o\on{T}J$, $\on{T}J	o\on{T}A$. By weight reasons, the composition $\hz(1)	o\on{T}J	o\on{T}A$ is trivial, hence we have a factorization $\hz(1)	o\on{T}T	o\on{T}J$. We have a short exact sequence \[\projlim_{n}A[n](k_{
u})	o\H^{1}(k_{
u},\on{T}T)	o\H^{1}(k_{
u},\on{T}J).\] By Mattuck's theorem \cite{mat55} $A(k_{
u})$ has finite torsion, thus $\H^{1}(k_{
u},\on{T}T)	o\H^{1}(k_{
u},\on{T}J)$ is injective. The hypothesis implies that the image of $\H^{1}(k,\hz(1))$ in $\H^{1}(k_{
