n_{\alpha}
n_{ eta}. \] Therefore, the morphism \[ \vartheta\colon H^2(X,{\mathbb Z})_{m tr} \lra \hh^ ot \subset H^2(\Sigma,{\mathbb Z}), \qquad \alpha\longmapsto 
n_{\alpha} \] gives an isometry $\vartheta_{{\mathbb C}}\colon H^2(X,{\mathbb Z})_{m tr}\otimes{\mathbb C} \isomto \hh^\perp\otimes{\mathbb C}$. The morphism $\vartheta$ is a nonzero morphism of Hodge structures.\ Since the Hodge structure~$H^2(X)_{m tr}$ is irreducible,~$\vartheta$ is injective hence $\Sigma$ has Picard number one.
